concepts.explanation:
A sequence is a set of instructions carried out in a fixed order, one after another, from top to bottom. When your program runs, the micro:bit reads the first line, does it, then reads the second line, does it, and so on. It never skips ahead and never guesses what you meant. This makes the order of your lines the single most important thing about a simple program.

Here is a tiny program that outputs the numbers 1, 2 and 3, with a one-second pause between each. The display.scroll() command scrolls text or numbers across the LED display, and sleep() pauses the program. Remember that time on the micro:bit is measured in milliseconds: there are 1000 milliseconds in one second, so sleep(1000) waits for one second and sleep(500) waits for half a second.

from microbit import * display.scroll(1) sleep(1000) display.scroll(2) sleep(1000) display.scroll(3)

A single picture is a still image. If you show one picture, wait, then show a different picture, your eyes see movement. That is exactly how every animation and every cartoon works, and it is how you create a light sequence on the micro:bit. The recipe is always the same: show an image, sleep, show the next image, sleep, and so on.

This program lights the left column, waits half a second, then lights the right column. The light appears to jump from one side to the other:

from microbit import * display.show(Image("90000:90000:90000:90000:90000")) # left column sleep(500) display.show(Image("00009:00009:00009:00009:00009")) # right column sleep(500) display.clear()

An alarm should only go off when it is supposed to. That means your program must make a decision — it must check a condition and choose what to do. Choosing between paths is called selection, and you write it with IF and ELSE. An input such as a button has just two states: pressed (True) or not pressed (False).

This program keeps checking button A. The while True: loop is what makes it keep checking; without it, the program would look at the button just once, at the very start, and then end. Notice the colon at the end of the if and else lines, and the indentation of the lines beneath them:

from microbit import * while True: if button_a.is_pressed(): display.show(Image.HAPPY) else: display.show(Image.SAD)

What the simulator does: while you hold button A down with the mouse, a happy face shows; when you release it, a sad face shows. Now, sometimes you need to combine more than one input. The Boolean operators AND, OR and NOT let you do this. They behave just like the logic gates you met earlier in the year.

from microbit import * while True: if button_a.is_pressed() and button_b.is_pressed(): display.show(Image.HAPPY) else: display.show(Image.SAD)

What the simulator does: a happy face appears only when you hold both A and B at the same time. Pressing just one shows the sad face. The word and sits between the two checks.

from microbit import * while True: if button_a.is_pressed() or button_b.is_pressed(): display.show(Image.HAPPY) else: display.show(Image.SAD)

What the simulator does: a happy face appears when you press A, or B, or both. Only when neither is pressed does the sad face show. This is exactly the kind of logic your alarm might use: trigger if the sound sensor hears something OR if someone presses a button.

NOT — the opposite of the input

from microbit import * while True: if not button_a.is_pressed(): display.show(Image.HAPPY) else: display.show(Image.SAD)
